To determine the number of possible integer values for \( x \) in the triangle, we analyze the triangle inequality conditions. 

First, we apply the triangle inequality theorem, which states that the sum of any two sides must be greater than the third side. This gives us three inequalities:

\[
\begin{aligned}
1. &\quad x + 13 > 37 \implies x > 24 \\
2. &\quad x + 37 > 13 \implies x > -24 \quad (\text{ignore since length can't be negative}) \\
3. &\quad 13 + 37 > x \implies x < 50 \\
\end{aligned}
\]

Combining these results, we find that \( 24 < x < 50 \). Therefore, the integer values \( x \) can take are 25, 26, ..., 49. Counting these gives us \( 49 - 25 + 1 = 25 \) integer values.
